  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "claim_3e1f9d796490409fb8568fd01a1eeeee",
      "statement": "Because the sample mean return has standard error SD/√T, historical expected returns are estimated too imprecisely to use raw: the S&P 500 1926–2014 (mean 12.0%, vol 20.1%, 89 years) yields a 95% interval of roughly 7.7%–16.3%, and on 2002–2014 alone the interval spans negative to positive, failing to establish even the sign.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/08_estimation_error_outliers_and_shrinkage.md#L28-L36",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:d5a368cbdf00fe159c397a50fc5721859d153e12ab768551c6b3737174b43f24:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_99445d42e8e74bde9c5b5e1098392242",
      "statement": "The required sample size for useful precision is T=(2σ/h)², so at σ=20.1% a ±2% half-width needs about 404 years and a ±1% half-width about 1616 years — explaining why the realized mean is not a reliable estimate of expected return.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/08_estimation_error_outliers_and_shrinkage.md#L40-L40",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:5d8e26b1338fc4b3e7d4a24d498a66869ce5fc687f02043870614afd08b68da1:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_7292a113b8844f0fa4ca01af4333b01b",
      "statement": "Mean-variance optimization amplifies input imprecision because it inverts the covariance matrix: when the number of assets p exceeds the sample size n the sample covariance is not invertible, and when p/n is non-negligibly below one it is ill-conditioned so inversion drastically amplifies estimation error.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/08_estimation_error_outliers_and_shrinkage.md#L44-L46",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:246f3cf74c8b89b180918ffa84e1a1a2fc88b3db80759114e0d8715df0835256:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_b4545a35ceae415dbadd748d40462db9",
      "statement": "A beta can be inferred reasonably accurately from as little as two years of data, whereas the historical mean return stays unreliable, motivating routing expected-return estimation through the risk–return relation rather than estimating single-stock means directly.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/08_estimation_error_outliers_and_shrinkage.md#L48-L48",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:edbf31e0cf0cabec23c9396e88fd4a435e1c19e1e9f120a2d0d7e898a3a5884b:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_3a3103724dbc4cb0bf5e963644d4c2b9",
      "statement": "Winsorization replaces an extreme observation with the value of its surviving neighbor rather than discarding it, grounded in censored-normal-sample estimation; Dixon's tabulated efficiencies never fall below 0.962 for the mean, so it costs negligible efficiency when data are truly normal.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/08_estimation_error_outliers_and_shrinkage.md#L54-L64",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:ba8f92a32126d4b5930e95d6300ecdc92214a0d8bcaeb3ad88e47af395184eee:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_de691867d831474d91d1b4dd45326808",
      "statement": "Shrinkage is justified by the bias–variance decomposition MSE = variance + squared bias: a structured target is all bias/no variance and the raw sample estimate is all variance/no bias, so their optimal combination trades one error type against the other, following the James–Stein idea.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/08_estimation_error_outliers_and_shrinkage.md#L68-L74",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:4515a787175e1f70967a063d4797dd156fa99fe0a72cf6a41d8f2066964d7352:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_f9b6494c4be3430ea3a1cd41e582f00b",
      "statement": "The precision-weighted Bayesian combination b'' = (b'/s_b'² + b/s_b²)/(1/s_b'² + 1/s_b²) pulls a raw estimate toward a prior target in proportion to the raw estimate's imprecision, with the combined precision equal to the sum of the two precisions.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/08_estimation_error_outliers_and_shrinkage.md#L76-L80",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:4959b695d793d373e5819520c40333b009a3b7ac901223e4072afcf8b5f0a371:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_9616281d9d2749808fb87465fad49b61",
      "statement": "Vasicek's Bayesian beta shrinkage uses the cross-sectional distribution of betas (concentrated near 1.0) as prior, giving a posterior mean that adjusts the OLS beta toward the prior in proportion to its precision and remains consistent as the sampling variance goes to zero.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/08_estimation_error_outliers_and_shrinkage.md#L90-L96",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:158eeda2f66f7410989a22e405988b43fb8c223b55b591b60b9667990625e6ed:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_ff3f03a90b044fc8847634c9bd4400ed",
      "statement": "Practitioner beta adjustments shrink toward one — Merrill Lynch's b''=1+k(b-1) and Bloomberg's Adjusted Beta = (2/3)β + (1/3)(1.0) — justified by suspicion of extreme estimates and Blume's empirical evidence that estimated betas regress toward 1.0 over time.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/08_estimation_error_outliers_and_shrinkage.md#L98-L106",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:718c1758f45410577345570f859cd33851f3107740e2733390d01a3c8b6853f5:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_130bea75c4b04aed9fb53186bd1a3e43",
      "statement": "Ledoit–Wolf linear shrinkage combines the sample covariance with a structured target (the identity scaled by average variance μI), giving Σ* = ρ·μI + (1−ρ)S with shrinkage intensity ρ = β²/δ², and the percentage relative improvement in loss (PRIAL) equals that intensity.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/08_estimation_error_outliers_and_shrinkage.md#L108-L124",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:a260108d41c9c8b20ebb82136c46592330b557b01d333eed5950cabb10bc13ca:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_a7182fefe4a94469b52a6846071c1c6b",
      "statement": "Sample eigenvalues are over-dispersed around their mean (largest biased up, smallest biased down) because of error in the sample eigenvectors, so Ledoit–Wolf linear shrinkage contracts every eigenvalue toward the mean μ by the same intensity.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/08_estimation_error_outliers_and_shrinkage.md#L126-L134",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:8294c1349395e203ddd59dc26a42f195597d33ac17bd0356180688253d482a7a:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_bf1c016747c748c893f826ba2bda6197",
      "statement": "The feasible linear-shrinkage estimator, under general asymptotics (p and n both to infinity with p/n bounded), is consistent, has asymptotically minimum quadratic risk among all linear combinations of the identity and the sample matrix, is always invertible even when p>n, and is typically well-conditioned.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/08_estimation_error_outliers_and_shrinkage.md#L136-L140",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:4fa5298fb156d61fe6cc5c06f43f87e451a4bf003b9034f2d041cc07894475fe:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_629d941c6ba147428ca171b7313ecad8",
      "statement": "Linear shrinkage is only the first-order approximation to a fundamentally non-linear problem; non-linear shrinkage applies an individualized intensity to each sample eigenvalue via a population-independent oracle depending only on the sample-eigenvalue Stieltjes transform, asymptotically matching and often beating linear shrinkage.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/08_estimation_error_outliers_and_shrinkage.md#L142-L162",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:be64165a8fbb750d20919d8f23fd00eadbd0b64594baddcf46d6593267075ed9:"
        }
      ]
    }
